  "output": "Good — that confirms it. Here's the honest picture: **most of the classic issues you asked about don't exist yet, because most of the code doesn't exist yet.** Rather than invent hypothetical bugs, here's what's actually there and what to watch for.\n\n## State of the codebase\n\n- **`web/`**: real code is a landing page, auth, and one CRUD screen (`sources`). Everything is a Server Component — zero `useEffect`/`useState`/`setInterval` anywhere in `web/app` or `web/components`.\n- **`engine/`**: `pipeline/blocking.py`, `pipeline/detect.py`, `db.py`, `agent/graph.py` are all `raise NotImplementedError(...)` stubs (Phase 2/3 per `AGENTS.md`). There is no matching, detection, or resolution logic to profile yet.\n\nSo: no N+1 SQL exists (no queries beyond one `SELECT`), no re-render bugs exist (no client state), no memory leaks exist (no subscriptions/timers). Below are the real, actionable items — one present-day fix, and design notes to bake in *before* Phase 2/3 land, since fixing this after the pipeline is built is much more expensive.\n\n## 1. Present-day: duplicate query, no request-level cache\n\n`web/lib/server/sources.ts:23-24`\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();   // full SELECT, then filters in JS\n  ...\n}\n```\n`get-started/page.tsx` calls `countSourcesBySide()`, `sources/page.tsx` calls `listSources()`. Next's App Router only dedupes `fetch()` per-request — a raw Drizzle call gets no automatic memoization. Today it's one extra query on one page; the moment a dashboard renders both a count widget and a table in the same request, that's 2x DB round-trips for identical data.\n\nFix now, cheaply, with React's `cache()`:\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n`countSourcesBySide` then reuses the memoized call for free within the same request.\n\n## 2. Design-ahead: engine writes (`db.py`), still stubs\n\nWhen `write_entities` / `write_matches` / `write_conflicts` get implemented, a run producing \"thousands of rows\" (per `AGENTS.md`) is the classic N+1 trap if written as a per-row `INSERT` loop with `psycopg`. Use `psycopg.extras`/`copy` or `executemany` with a single batched statement:\n```python\n# avoid\nfor e in entities:\n    cur.execute(\"INSERT INTO entities (...) VALUES (...)\", e)\n\n# do this\nwith cur.copy(\"COPY entities (...) FROM STDIN\") as copy:\n    for e in entities:\n        copy.write_row(e.as_tuple())\n```\n\n## 3. Design-ahead: resolution agent (`agent/graph.py`), still a stub\n\n`resolve()` will loop over conflicts and call Claude per-conflict — this is the LLM-equivalent of N+1. `AGENTS.md` already mandates the fix (prompt caching on the ruleset block, Batch API for `eval/` sweeps) — make sure the production `resolve()` path also caches the ruleset block across calls in a run, not just in eval. Concretely: build the cached system-prompt block once per run (outside the per-conflict loop), reuse it across every Claude call in that run.\n\n## 4. Design-ahead: `blocking.py`, still a stub\n\nComment already flags \"never compare all pairs\" — good. When implementing `block()`, build the FAISS index once per run (`IndexFlatIP`/`IndexIVFFlat` over all of side B), not once per query entity. Building it per-lookup would silently reintroduce O(n²).\n\n## 5. Watch this when TanStack Query polling ships\n\n`AGENTS.md` specifies TanStack Query for run-status polling — none exists yet, but it's the one place a real memory leak will show up first. When you build it, make sure `refetchInterval` stops once `status` reaches a terminal state (`complete`/`failed`), otherwise a run detail page left open polls forever:\n```ts\nuseQuery({\n  queryKey: [\"run\", runId, \"status\"],\n  queryFn: () => fetchRunStatus(runId),\n  refetchInterval: (query) =>\n    query.state.data?.status && [\"complete\", \"failed\"].includes(query.state.data.status)\n      ? false\n      : 2000,\n});\n```\n\n## Bottom line\n\nNothing to fix urgently besides #1. The rest is guidance to apply *while writing* Phase 2/3, since AGENTS.md's own build order (`generate.py` → pipeline → agent → console) means these stubs will be filled in soon and the patterns above are cheap to get right from the start, expensive to retrofit after `eval/harness.py` numbers depend on them.\n",
